WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.890
- Welcome everyone to
the Jackson Institute.

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:15.820
I'm Ted Wittenstein, Deputy
Director for Leadership Programs

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:19.400
and we really have an extraordinary guest,

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:20.770
personal hero of mine,

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:24.140
someone who has traveled
from very far away

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:27.780
to be with us today at Yale, from Manila,

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.810
Ann Maria Ressa, who I will
introduce in just a moment,

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:33.170
along with Ambassador Harry Thomas,

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:36.290
one of our Senior Fellows
here at the Jackson Institute,

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:38.220
and someone who was instrumental

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:41.820
in bringing this extraordinary
advocate for human rights

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:43.180
to the Yale campus,

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:46.530
and we welcome her as a
Jackson Visiting Fellow

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:49.240
for the talk today on
"Journalism and Human Rights:

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:53.050
Fighting Back Against Disinformation."

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:54.240
I think many of you are familiar

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.700
with the Jackson Visiting Fellows Program.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:00.390
These are distinguished
practitioners and global affairs,

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:01.950
who are invited to spend

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:05.550
an immersive multi-day
program on the Yale campus.

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:07.150
Many of you have seen and interacted

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:10.290
with some of the visiting
Fellows in this past semester,

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:14.160
including former NATO Commander,
Admiral Jim Stavridis,

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.620
former Commander of US Central Command,

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.460
General David Petraeus.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:22.950
Maria Ressa is not a
soldier, but make no mistake,

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:27.950
she is a warrior, fearless,
tireless defender of democracy,

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:34.280
a champion of transparency,
government accountability,

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.440
freedom of speech and the press,

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:39.210
both in the Philippines
and around the world,

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:43.310
and often in the face
of great personal risk.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:47.470
She has been a journalist in
Asia for more than 30 years.

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:50.730
In 2012, she co-founded rappler.com,

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.920
which is now one of the
leading news organizations

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:53.810
in the Philippines.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:54.970
And prior to that,

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:59.650
served as CNN's Bureau
Chief in Manila and Jakarta.

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:02.400
But that's does not do justice

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.550
to what the work that she is
doing in the Philippines today

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:08.930
and the way that that
resonates around the world.

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:12.210
When I was sitting in
coffee with Maria yesterday,

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.800
she remarked how nice it was to be sitting

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:19.800
and looking out a window
and not fearing sniper fire.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:23.320
So the work that she is doing is very real

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:28.320
and very risky in defense of
world values and human rights.

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:31.810
She travels with great
security in the Philippines,

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:35.230
she has to train her
staff on how to respond

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:38.900
in the event of surprise
government arrest.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:40.030
As many of you know,

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:44.080
her reporting on
authoritarian administration

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.680
of President Rodrigo Duterte,

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:49.790
has resulted in repeated
intimidation tactics

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:54.420
and a number of manufactured
attempts to detain her.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:58.670
But she has fought back
against this disinformation

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:02.980
and abuse of power with
great gusto and enthusiasm

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:06.570
in a way that has inspired so many others.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.840
She has said we are journalists

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:11.390
and we will not be intimidated.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:15.700
We will shine the light,
we will hold the line.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:20.490
And it is this courageous and
deeply inspirational vision

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.760
that has resonated worldwide
that has earned her recognition

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:26.770
as Time magazine Person
of the Year in 2018,

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:29.280
along with numerous other accolades.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:30.280
And we're delighted

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:34.120
to have Ambassador Harry Thomas
moderate this conversation.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:37.260
Ambassador Thomas served as
Ambassador to the Philippines

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:39.170
from 2010 to 2013.

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:42.940
He witnessed very racist
efforts firsthand,

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:47.410
he also served as Ambassador
to Bangladesh and Zimbabwe,

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:49.830
along with numerous other senior positions

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:51.630
in the US foreign service.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:54.120
We're delighted to have Ambassador Thomas

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:57.970
as a Senior Fellow at the
Jackson Institute at Yale.

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.610
So Maria Ressa has a brief presentation

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:02.940
highlighting some of the
key work that she's done

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:06.560
in countering authoritarianism
and disinformation.

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:10.630
After which she'll join the
stage with Ambassador Thomas

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:13.030
for a conversation between the two of you

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:15.830
and an interactive Q&amp;A with the audience.

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:19.090
So please join me in thanking Maria Ressa,

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:22.350
welcome her from the
Philippines to New Haven,

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:25.202
to the Jackson Institute and
to Yale, thank you very much.

00:04:25.202 --> 00:04:28.202
(audience clapping)

00:04:37.523 --> 00:04:41.273
(Maria speaking off the mic)

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:47.800
- Yay, I will run for
about 15 to 18 minutes

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:49.463
and then kinda take you through.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.250
Here are the three points.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:55.120
The three points I hope
to make in this one

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:56.290
and it's so good to see

00:04:56.290 --> 00:04:59.200
so many familiar faces in the audience.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:00.200
What's the problem?

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:02.770
What's the problem and why should you care

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:04.033
about the Philippines?

00:05:05.315 --> 00:05:09.617
Why is our dystopian present
your dystopian future?

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:13.080
And what are the potential solutions?

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:14.713
The problem is very simple.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:19.760
It is the impact of
technology as an accelerant,

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:23.400
that has helped elect authoritarian
populist-style leaders

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:28.400
that have used the technology
to actually make lies

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:34.600
laced with anger and hate
spread faster than facts.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.660
Without facts, you can't have truth.

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:40.120
Without truth, you can't have trust.

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:43.443
Without any of these, democracy
as we know it is dead.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:48.200
2020 is a critical year I think.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:50.430
I hope you guys do really well here

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:55.420
because what happens here
we'll have a global impact.

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:57.170
Why the Philippines?

00:05:57.170 --> 00:06:00.200
Because this is the fourth year in a row

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:02.650
that the Philippines is
the social media capital.

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:09.170
100% of Filipinos are on the
internet, 100% are on Facebook.

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:11.153
Facebook is our internet.

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:15.640
I interviewed Cambridge Analytica
whistleblower, Chris Wiley

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:18.560
and I asked him, "What role
did the Philippines play?"

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:20.170
He called it the Petri dish.

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:22.310
He said in countries like ours,

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:23.990
because of the young population.

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:27.330
100 million people, median
age is 23 years old.

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:30.080
Because of that, they tested these tactics

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:33.210
of mass manipulation in our countries

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:35.853
and if it worked on us,
they brought it to you.

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:39.293
They brought it to the
United States to Europe.

00:06:40.996 --> 00:06:42.950
So Petri dish is his word,

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:47.930
and he said that if it
worked in the countries

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:50.350
like the Philippines and Nigeria,

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:53.873
that they would then
port the tactics over.

00:06:54.730 --> 00:06:58.203
The third part is, I'm not
gonna leave you depressed.

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:02.490
And all So you need to
be extremely empowered,

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:05.560
because you're also a frontline, right?

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:07.990
Some of my problems in our country,

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:10.740
my personal problems,
the attacks against me,

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:15.740
come and happen on American
social media platforms.

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:19.500
Part of what's changed the
entire information ecosystem

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:22.930
is because American social media platforms

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:25.680
who allegedly have the
same values of democracy

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:27.030
that we all have,

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:30.280
is they moved fast and
they broke democracy.

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:32.223
So I hope I'm wrong.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:35.260
So what is it?

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:36.613
The perfect example is,

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:41.890
in 2018, the Philippine
government filed 11 cases

00:07:41.890 --> 00:07:43.630
and investigations against me.

00:07:43.630 --> 00:07:44.913
I think that was why,

00:07:46.801 --> 00:07:51.460
when I was one of four
covers that they had in 2018,

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:54.010
when I saw this, I
thought it was fake news

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:56.433
and forwarded it to our
social media platform,

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:01.620
to our team because I was like,
"Oh, they didn't tell me."

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:04.700
And then as soon as I
got a call telling me,

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:08.810
it was CNN asking for a
response, my stomach sank

00:08:08.810 --> 00:08:11.697
because I thought that meant that,

00:08:11.697 --> 00:08:13.780
"Oh my gosh will be attacked again."

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:15.480
But you can't get attacked
more than we have.

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:17.820
Although of course, the
number of cases can go up.

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:21.833
In 2018, because of the cases
that were filed against us,

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:25.650
our revenue dropped almost 50%.

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:30.210
The end goal is to really bankrupt us,

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:31.820
it's a war of attrition.

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:35.750
In 2019. my Valentine's Day
gift from the Government

00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:39.193
is to be arrested and
then to be held overnight,

00:08:40.055 --> 00:08:42.270
and then within five weeks
to be arrested again.

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:45.310
In order to be here today.
I've posted bail five,

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:47.020
sorry, eight times.

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:48.870
I've posted bail eight times

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:53.530
and I have paid more in bail
and bonds than Imelda Marcos,

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:56.203
who's been convicted in
four different countries.

00:08:58.946 --> 00:09:02.640
My lawyer says that one of my
cases, the cyber libel case,

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:05.600
it's a case where we
published a story in 2012,

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:07.090
four months before the law

00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:10.500
we allegedly violated was enacted.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:15.080
This case now began in August 2019

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:18.940
and we'll have a verdict on
April third, please keep track.

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:21.620
I could go to jail for
12 years on that one.

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:25.590
The guardians of truth,
the guardians of truth.

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:28.780
And on the four covers, I was
the only one at that point

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:31.030
who was both alive and free.

00:09:31.030 --> 00:09:33.030
This is what it takes to
be a journalist today,

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:35.273
that's crazy, right?

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:38.726
Here's the problem.

00:09:38.726 --> 00:09:42.540
It's nice to meet Tim Snyder
in the hallway here yesterday

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:46.880
because I use this slide
almost in every time I speak.

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:48.750
I talked about the problem like this,

00:09:48.750 --> 00:09:50.990
if you can make people
believe lies are the facts,

00:09:50.990 --> 00:09:52.470
then you can control them.

00:09:52.470 --> 00:09:53.870
Tim says it's so much better

00:09:53.870 --> 00:09:56.980
and I recommend his book,
"On Tyranny", to everyone,

00:09:56.980 --> 00:09:59.673
because it gave me the political,

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:03.520
if we can contain the pollution

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:05.650
in our information ecosystem,

00:10:05.650 --> 00:10:07.920
maybe we're only looking
at one or two years

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:10.330
of this dysfunctional democracy.

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:13.030
But if we don't, then
we're looking at decades.

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:18.030
And Tim actually lays out
why and how in "On Tyranny".

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:20.290
He said, if you wanna rip
the heart out of a democracy,

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:21.770
you go after facts.

00:10:21.770 --> 00:10:23.980
That's what modern authoritarians do.

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:26.720
Step one, you lie all the time.

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:28.650
Step two, you say it's your opponents

00:10:28.650 --> 00:10:30.320
and the journalists who lie.

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:33.077
Step three, everyone
looks around and says,

00:10:33.077 --> 00:10:36.590
"What's the truth?"
There is no truth, right?

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:38.140
Because if there is no truth,

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:40.900
resistance is impossible
and the game is over.

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:42.790
What does civic engagement look like

00:10:42.790 --> 00:10:46.060
in the age of information warfare?

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:51.060
This time proves power and
information, information is power

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:53.373
and now more than anything,

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:58.823
the Muller Report lays out
Russian disinformation.

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:04.270
I wonder why we keep letting impunity

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:07.050
reign in our information ecosystem?

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:10.000
Here's what we went
through in the Philippines.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:13.050
In 2017, we started to do a report

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:16.520
along with 12 other research
groups, with a Think Tank.

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:19.530
We came out with the phrase
"Patriotic trolling",

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:21.240
state-sponsored online hate

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:24.170
and harassment campaigns
to silence and intimidate.

00:11:24.170 --> 00:11:27.450
They attack you, pound you to silence,

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:29.540
so they use freedom of speech

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:32.740
and expression to stifle a narrative.

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:35.730
If you're pounded to silence,
that's the first step.

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:40.730
The second step is they come
and actually incite hate

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:44.483
and violence against you,
those are the two tactics.

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:47.370
They flood the market with lies.

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:49.580
Instead of actually trying to censor,

00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:50.950
you flood the market with lies.

00:11:50.950 --> 00:11:54.940
Women are attacked in the
Philippines at least 10 times more

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:57.790
and these were the three steps

00:11:57.790 --> 00:12:01.340
because a senator of ours, Lila de Lima,

00:12:01.340 --> 00:12:04.150
she's a first-term senator,
but a former Justice Secretary.

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:06.470
She's the former Head of the
Commission on Human Rights.

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:08.790
She's been in prison for three years now

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:11.330
without any real trial,

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:14.130
and she went through these steps as well.

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:17.620
The first is an attack on
credibility that I mentioned.

00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:20.040
It's her and me and our Vice President,

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:21.280
who's the head of the opposition.

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:23.920
We were the three cases that came through.

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:26.280
Attack the credibility,
you alleged corruption,

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:29.020
you say it, repeat it exponentially.

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:31.463
A lie told a million times becomes a fact.

00:12:33.050 --> 00:12:35.850
You sexual violence, number two.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:40.850
There was video of her
supposedly, in a sexual act.

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:42.200
It was doctored,

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:44.670
but a Congressman actually
threatened to play it

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:46.333
at the House of Representatives.

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:50.940
The minute you're degraded
as a sexual object,

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:53.390
it's very hard to retain your credibility.

00:12:53.390 --> 00:12:56.800
I think I've been called
every animal you can think of,

00:12:56.800 --> 00:13:00.030
you develop a thick skin but
I'll show you some of it.

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:02.410
Then the last part is,
before she was arrested,

00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:05.933
a few weeks before, they
trended #ArrestLeiladeLima.

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:10.670
In my case,

00:13:10.670 --> 00:13:15.620
they tried to trend
#ArrestMariaRessa in May of 2017.

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:18.530
It didn't trend, which is
probably why it took two years

00:13:18.530 --> 00:13:19.733
before they arrested me.

00:13:21.390 --> 00:13:25.200
This is #MariaRessa
arrest, it was in May 2017.

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:26.770
We came out with the story

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:29.600
of the conversation
between Trump and Duterte,

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:30.900
and we weren't the only ones,

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:32.240
the Washington had posted it as well.

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:34.210
But this is a content creator,

00:13:34.210 --> 00:13:36.280
a blogger who now works
for the government.

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:39.770
But at that point, he was one
of the pro-Duterte bloggers.

00:13:39.770 --> 00:13:42.377
He writes this, #ArrestMariaRessa,

00:13:43.286 --> 00:13:44.430
Rappler just made the Philippines

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:47.730
a legitimate target of North
Korean nuclear missiles.

00:13:47.730 --> 00:13:49.280
It's ridiculous right?

00:13:49.280 --> 00:13:51.920
And yet, people think it's real.

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:55.270
It's spread to here,
Twitter, campaign accounts.

00:13:55.270 --> 00:13:56.660
This is a normal process,

00:13:56.660 --> 00:13:58.640
they take social media campaign accounts,

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:01.790
and then pivot and weaponize
it after taking power.

00:14:01.790 --> 00:14:03.896
That's what happened in the Philippines.

00:14:03.896 --> 00:14:08.500
(mumbles) call her to the
Senate, #ArrestMariaRessa.

00:14:08.500 --> 00:14:10.593
Then it jumps to a real person.

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:15.110
I can smell an arrest and
possible closure of rappler.com.

00:14:15.110 --> 00:14:16.520
This is made two years

00:14:16.520 --> 00:14:18.630
before the government
tried to close us down.

00:14:18.630 --> 00:14:21.060
So you can kind of see this astroturfing.

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:25.940
You see the idea first on
social media bottom up, right?

