00:28:08.330 --> 00:28:11.393
that allowed us to grow
this revenue stream.

00:28:13.380 --> 00:28:15.260
I'll leave you with these.

00:28:15.260 --> 00:28:16.540
This is what our world is today.

00:28:16.540 --> 00:28:19.440
Tim Snyder said it here, rip
the heart out of a democracy.

00:28:19.440 --> 00:28:22.217
But this is the situation
in the Philippines.

00:28:22.217 --> 00:28:24.507
"Combined with online and offline violence

00:28:24.507 --> 00:28:26.230
"to create a climate of fear."

00:28:26.230 --> 00:28:27.533
It is a climate of fear.

00:28:28.660 --> 00:28:31.077
We're being live-streamed,
I will tell you this.

00:28:31.077 --> 00:28:33.097
"Anyone who questions a brutal drug war,

00:28:33.097 --> 00:28:35.227
"supports those who fight for truth.

00:28:35.227 --> 00:28:36.837
"Attack them with the full force

00:28:36.837 --> 00:28:38.240
"of the Philippine Government.

00:28:38.240 --> 00:28:40.400
Lila de Lima has been
in jail for three years

00:28:40.400 --> 00:28:41.683
without a proper trial.

00:28:44.540 --> 00:28:45.900
I don't know what I am.

00:28:45.900 --> 00:28:47.430
Someone actually told me,

00:28:47.430 --> 00:28:50.170
I don't have to be in prison right now.

00:28:50.170 --> 00:28:52.880
Because in a way, I already am in prison.

00:28:52.880 --> 00:28:55.040
It depends, it's like a game of chicken.

00:28:55.040 --> 00:28:57.290
How far do you go?
You don't know.

00:28:57.290 --> 00:29:00.190
And it took a while to actually tell me

00:29:00.190 --> 00:29:04.410
all of the charges I face,
if I lose all of them,

00:29:04.410 --> 00:29:06.963
the maximum sentence
is 83 years in prison.

00:29:07.860 --> 00:29:10.740
You make them cautionary
tales for demanding the truth.

00:29:10.740 --> 00:29:12.870
So what do we do, what do we do?

00:29:12.870 --> 00:29:14.640
We start with the area of influence,

00:29:14.640 --> 00:29:16.530
you demand accountability from power.

00:29:16.530 --> 00:29:17.730
We continue to do that.

00:29:17.730 --> 00:29:22.030
if each of us does that we put
our patch of ground together,

00:29:22.030 --> 00:29:24.990
stand up against bullies, report the lies,

00:29:24.990 --> 00:29:26.120
demand accountability

00:29:26.120 --> 00:29:28.410
from your American social media platforms.

00:29:28.410 --> 00:29:32.180
Those are my two things that
I demand, an end to impunity,

00:29:32.180 --> 00:29:36.017
our government on the
drug war and Facebook.

00:29:36.017 --> 00:29:38.197
"Tell your family and
friends, courage spreads.

00:29:38.197 --> 00:29:41.170
"We begin by taking care
of what's in front of us."

00:29:41.170 --> 00:29:43.820
I'm gonna leave you, it isn't
just Rappler who's doing this.

00:29:43.820 --> 00:29:46.080
It is every Filipino journalist.

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:48.250
Journalists are under attack globally.

00:29:48.250 --> 00:29:50.680
And I'll leave you with
a minute and a half video

00:29:50.680 --> 00:29:54.580
that shows you some of the
things we have to deal with.

00:29:54.580 --> 00:29:57.810
- [Interviewer] You ever been
harassed because of your work?

00:29:57.810 --> 00:29:58.643
- Yes.

00:29:58.643 --> 00:30:00.798
- [Interviewer] Have you
been threatened online?

00:30:00.798 --> 00:30:01.631
(speaks in foreign language)

00:30:01.631 --> 00:30:02.960
Have you been called biased?

00:30:02.960 --> 00:30:03.793
- Yes.

00:30:03.793 --> 00:30:05.390
- [Interviewer] Have
you been called stupid?

00:30:05.390 --> 00:30:07.750
- Yes, many times by idiots.

00:30:07.750 --> 00:30:09.530
- [Interviewer] Have you
been called disrespectful?

00:30:09.530 --> 00:30:10.363
- Yes.

00:30:10.363 --> 00:30:12.400
- [Interviewer] Have you
been accused of corruption?

00:30:12.400 --> 00:30:13.330
- Yes.

00:30:13.330 --> 00:30:15.200
- [Interviewer] Have you been called ugly

00:30:15.200 --> 00:30:17.310
as a response to any story?

00:30:17.310 --> 00:30:18.143
- Yes.

00:30:18.143 --> 00:30:19.860
- [Interviewer] Have you
been called fake news?

00:30:19.860 --> 00:30:22.400
- Oh, yeah, they always say I'm fake news.

00:30:22.400 --> 00:30:24.700
Anything that's critical is fake, right?

00:30:24.700 --> 00:30:25.980
- [Interviewer] Have you been accused

00:30:25.980 --> 00:30:28.353
of being an imperialist spy?

00:30:28.353 --> 00:30:29.186
- Yes.

00:30:29.186 --> 00:30:30.190
- [Interviewer] Have you been accused

00:30:30.190 --> 00:30:32.050
of being a communist operative?

00:30:32.050 --> 00:30:32.883
- Yes.

00:30:32.883 --> 00:30:33.716
- [Interviewer] Have you been accused

00:30:33.716 --> 00:30:35.280
of working for the CIA?

00:30:35.280 --> 00:30:36.113
- Yes.

00:30:36.113 --> 00:30:37.440
- [Interviewer] Have you
been sexually harassed

00:30:37.440 --> 00:30:38.690
as a journalist?

00:30:38.690 --> 00:30:39.523
- Yes.

00:30:39.523 --> 00:30:40.940
- [Interviewer] Has your
family been threatened,

00:30:40.940 --> 00:30:43.010
harassed or alluded to?

00:30:43.010 --> 00:30:46.943
- Yes, it has specifically
my daughter when she died.

00:30:47.790 --> 00:30:49.760
There were a lot of people
who made fun of that.

00:30:49.760 --> 00:30:51.520
- [Interviewer] Have you
been threatened with rape?

00:30:51.520 --> 00:30:52.710
- Yes.

00:30:52.710 --> 00:30:53.543
- Yes.

00:30:53.543 --> 00:30:55.450
- No, not me but my family.

00:30:55.450 --> 00:30:57.680
- [Interviewer] Have you been
threatened with violence?

00:30:57.680 --> 00:30:58.513
- Yes.

00:30:58.513 --> 00:31:00.160
- [Interviewer] Have you
been threatened with death?

00:31:00.160 --> 00:31:01.010
- Yes.

00:31:01.010 --> 00:31:02.080
- [Interviewer] Have you been told

00:31:02.080 --> 00:31:04.030
how you're going to be killed?

00:31:04.030 --> 00:31:04.863
- Yes.

00:31:04.863 --> 00:31:06.910
- [Interviewer] Has the
violence been described to you?

00:31:06.910 --> 00:31:10.430
- Yeah, blow my head off or bury me alive.

00:31:10.430 --> 00:31:12.794
- [Interviewer] What will
stop you from reporting?

00:31:12.794 --> 00:31:13.788
- Nothing.

00:31:13.788 --> 00:31:14.621
- Nothing.

00:31:14.621 --> 00:31:15.740
- Nothing.

00:31:15.740 --> 00:31:16.573
- Death.

00:31:16.573 --> 00:31:17.870
- Dude, you have to kill me.

00:31:20.980 --> 00:31:22.083
- We hold the line.

00:31:23.923 --> 00:31:26.923
(audience clapping)

00:31:46.410 --> 00:31:47.755
- Good evening.

00:31:47.755 --> 00:31:50.540
(speaking in foreign language)

00:31:50.540 --> 00:31:54.433
Welcome everyone, I think
we were all impressed.

00:31:55.399 --> 00:31:57.760
I'm going to start off as a moderator

00:31:57.760 --> 00:31:59.990
with just a couple of questions,

00:31:59.990 --> 00:32:03.860
and then we really would
like to hear from the guests,

00:32:03.860 --> 00:32:05.720
especially the students,

00:32:05.720 --> 00:32:10.203
as they asked my good friend
Maria Ressa questions.

00:32:11.120 --> 00:32:13.030
And I learned a lot from Maria

00:32:13.030 --> 00:32:14.793
during my time in the Philippines.

00:32:16.860 --> 00:32:17.710
She wrote a book.

00:32:19.580 --> 00:32:22.330
Facebook have been
long, 10 days, 10 years,

00:32:22.330 --> 00:32:25.770
about terrorism and kidnapping,

00:32:25.770 --> 00:32:30.060
ABS CBN (mumbles) she
worked with reporters

00:32:30.060 --> 00:32:33.880
and southern Philippines and
how they work to return them.

00:32:33.880 --> 00:32:36.510
I was so impressed by the book

00:32:36.510 --> 00:32:39.610
that I invited her to my residence

00:32:39.610 --> 00:32:44.610
to talk to my team about
what you needed to learn.

00:32:46.410 --> 00:32:51.230
Like many of our fellow,
my fellow diplomats,

00:32:51.230 --> 00:32:54.730
Ted and and (mumbles),
we thought we knew a lot.

00:32:54.730 --> 00:32:56.090
Well, I realized what I did,

00:32:56.090 --> 00:32:57.900
I thought I knew a lot
about the Philippines

00:32:57.900 --> 00:33:01.820
until I married a Filipina and
realized I did not know much.

00:33:01.820 --> 00:33:05.103
And that's the way it
is in many countries.

00:33:06.875 --> 00:33:09.820
You have to have to learn
and you have to be brave.

00:33:09.820 --> 00:33:14.643
So my first question Maria, is why stay?

00:33:16.570 --> 00:33:20.663
- Oh wow, the basic questions
are the hardest, right?

00:33:23.993 --> 00:33:25.430
I think I have to stay, right?

00:33:25.430 --> 00:33:27.360
I mean, it kind of is horrible,

00:33:27.360 --> 00:33:30.700
that we come of age at different times.

00:33:30.700 --> 00:33:34.360
And I'm now a senior
journalist in the Philippines

00:33:34.360 --> 00:33:35.850
and the baton was passed to me

00:33:35.850 --> 00:33:38.940
at a time that's really
horrific for journalists.

00:33:38.940 --> 00:33:41.700
But I think that I wouldn't be me

00:33:41.700 --> 00:33:43.740
if I didn't find it head on.

00:33:43.740 --> 00:33:46.700
Like, it's easy to say you
have standards and ethics,

00:33:46.700 --> 00:33:50.000
it's easy to say you believe
in the mission of journalism,

00:33:50.000 --> 00:33:54.053
but if you duck when it
matters, then you don't.

00:33:54.983 --> 00:33:57.000
That kind of played around with my head,

00:33:57.000 --> 00:33:58.490
'cause I did think about it.

00:33:58.490 --> 00:34:01.690
I'm both an American
and a Filipino citizen.

00:34:01.690 --> 00:34:04.393
My family is so adamant,

00:34:05.971 --> 00:34:07.900
so I asked my mom to leave the Philippines

00:34:07.900 --> 00:34:09.110
and come back to the States.

00:34:09.110 --> 00:34:11.810
But the reality is that I
don't think I have a choice.

00:34:12.724 --> 00:34:16.180
You have to hold the line
because when you give it up,

00:34:16.180 --> 00:34:19.070
then you voluntarily give up your rights.

00:34:19.070 --> 00:34:22.253
Guys, don't voluntarily
give up your rights.

00:34:25.540 --> 00:34:27.090
- Thank you very much for that.

00:34:28.220 --> 00:34:31.040
Philippines for all its wandering beauty

00:34:31.040 --> 00:34:32.560
has the last decade,

00:34:32.560 --> 00:34:34.960
been the most dangerous
country for journalists,

00:34:36.780 --> 00:34:41.773
and yet you are encouraging
young people to enter journals.

00:34:43.010 --> 00:34:45.063
How and why do you do this?

00:34:47.030 --> 00:34:50.147
- I hate when he's
asking questions, right?

00:34:50.147 --> 00:34:51.800
(audience clapping)

00:34:51.800 --> 00:34:54.333
I used to do this and it
was more fun asking him,

00:34:56.710 --> 00:34:59.580
because, I think you saw, right?

00:34:59.580 --> 00:35:02.950
Our information ecosystem,
creative destruction,

00:35:02.950 --> 00:35:04.470
it's already been destroyed.

00:35:04.470 --> 00:35:07.110
And so what we're doing in Rappler

00:35:07.110 --> 00:35:09.810
is trying to deal with that destruction,

00:35:09.810 --> 00:35:11.860
journalists have lost
our gate keeping powers,

00:35:11.860 --> 00:35:14.360
we're no longer gatekeepers,
it went to tech.

00:35:14.360 --> 00:35:17.890
And tech has decided
that politicians can lie.

00:35:20.460 --> 00:35:23.050
That's actually what Mark
Zuckerberg said, right?

00:35:23.050 --> 00:35:25.400
And so this is the time

00:35:25.400 --> 00:35:29.490
when after the old world
we know has been destroyed,

00:35:29.490 --> 00:35:34.210
you come in and you create
what the new world is gonna be.

00:35:34.210 --> 00:35:35.710
That's incredible, right?

00:35:35.710 --> 00:35:39.363
And that's part of the reason
I stay and I work every day.

00:35:40.859 --> 00:35:41.960
I used to think when I was in CNN,

00:35:41.960 --> 00:35:44.110
I work really hard at 24 hour news.

00:35:44.110 --> 00:35:48.310
Forget that, Rappler is like
every hour of every day,

00:35:48.310 --> 00:35:50.200
it's 150% of what you want.

00:35:50.200 --> 00:35:55.200
But part of it is because, and
I'll toss this back to you,

00:35:55.500 --> 00:35:58.450
this is the time that matters.

00:35:58.450 --> 00:36:00.520
Everything that we grew up with,

00:36:00.520 --> 00:36:03.980
everything that we knew is under attack.

00:36:03.980 --> 00:36:06.163
Our democracy is under attack.

00:36:07.010 --> 00:36:08.240
When journalists are under attack,

00:36:08.240 --> 00:36:09.800
democracy is under attack.

00:36:09.800 --> 00:36:14.400
And so I feel like what we do matters.

00:36:14.400 --> 00:36:17.640
And for the young journalists
here in this room,

00:36:17.640 --> 00:36:20.160
when you jump in now, you're
not gonna do what I did

00:36:20.160 --> 00:36:21.600
when I started as a reporter,

00:36:21.600 --> 00:36:24.300
which is to follow what
someone else has done.

00:36:24.300 --> 00:36:27.340
You'll actually be creating new things.

00:36:27.340 --> 00:36:31.160
And the better you
understand data, technology,

00:36:31.160 --> 00:36:34.370
and society, behavioral science,

00:36:34.370 --> 00:36:36.840
the better your journalism will be.

00:36:36.840 --> 00:36:38.100
- Thank you very much.

00:36:38.100 --> 00:36:42.910
Now we'll ask everyone who
is going to make a question,

00:36:42.910 --> 00:36:45.783
please identify yourself and
keep your question brief.

00:36:49.061 --> 00:36:49.894
And it will go around,

00:36:49.894 --> 00:36:53.620
but I would I would first
like to give the opportunity

00:36:53.620 --> 00:36:56.163
to two, one new friend, one old friend.

00:36:57.400 --> 00:37:00.230
Vicki Acevedo is here tonight,

00:37:00.230 --> 00:37:03.130
she was in the second
class of Women at Yale,

00:37:03.130 --> 00:37:04.390
went on to Harvard Law School

00:37:04.390 --> 00:37:07.480
and has had a distinguished
career and we welcome her.

00:37:07.480 --> 00:37:10.077
I'd like to know, Vicki if
you have a question (mumbles).

00:37:13.980 --> 00:37:15.050
- [Vicki] Let me just first of all,

00:37:15.050 --> 00:37:17.490
say thank you very much
for that presentation.

00:37:17.490 --> 00:37:20.653
I mean, the big data
issue is so important.

00:37:21.650 --> 00:37:26.363
I just been wondering about
the revelation of truth.

00:37:28.000 --> 00:37:32.500
I watch CNN or CNBC or PBS,

00:37:32.500 --> 00:37:37.250
and they have consistently
pointed out the lies,

00:37:37.250 --> 00:37:41.260
all the time, but it doesn't
seem to make any difference.

00:37:41.260 --> 00:37:44.100
So I wonder if you could address that.

00:37:44.100 --> 00:37:45.360
You partially did

00:37:45.360 --> 00:37:48.020
in talking about people
who put their heads down,

00:37:48.020 --> 00:37:52.810
but why is it that
revealing the lying behavior

00:37:52.810 --> 00:37:56.890
doesn't change the citizens behavior?

00:37:56.890 --> 00:37:58.470
- So I think two reasons.

00:37:58.470 --> 00:38:02.047
The first is, Daniel Kahneman's book,

00:38:02.047 --> 00:38:04.223
"Thinking Fast, Thinking Slow", right?

00:38:05.160 --> 00:38:07.600
Our old world was thinking slow.

00:38:07.600 --> 00:38:12.600
Journalists rational thinking
stood a chance in that world,

00:38:12.690 --> 00:38:14.113
because we valued it.

00:38:15.870 --> 00:38:18.900
Those were the rules in
the systems we created.

00:38:18.900 --> 00:38:23.200
The new world rule and enabled by tech,

00:38:23.200 --> 00:38:27.220
is actually driven by
thinking fast, right?

00:38:27.220 --> 00:38:29.003
It's a Thinking Fast world,

00:38:31.240 --> 00:38:32.900
you can't call them anything

00:38:32.900 --> 00:38:35.920
but behavioral modification systems.

00:38:35.920 --> 00:38:39.800
Social media platforms are
behavioral modification systems

00:38:39.800 --> 00:38:42.833
and we are Pavlov's dogs walking into it.

00:38:45.900 --> 00:38:49.640
The wrong behavior is rewarded, right?

00:38:49.640 --> 00:38:54.640
So Mark will say that it
is about freedom of speech.

00:38:54.670 --> 00:38:56.510
It isn't about freedom of speech,

00:38:56.510 --> 00:39:00.870
because you can say anything
you want but not everything,

00:39:00.870 --> 00:39:03.050
and especially when it's violence.

00:39:03.050 --> 00:39:06.500
Not everything should
have wide distribution.

00:39:06.500 --> 00:39:09.640
And in the social media platforms,

00:39:09.640 --> 00:39:14.140
what actually gets
distributed faster and further

00:39:14.140 --> 00:39:19.140
are incendiary comments, lies
laced with anger and hate.

00:39:20.020 --> 00:39:22.290
You know it, it's part of who we are.

00:39:22.290 --> 00:39:26.190
It's almost like the devil and
the angel on our shoulders,

00:39:26.190 --> 00:39:28.330
except the devil keeps winning

00:39:28.330 --> 00:39:32.710
because the the platform itself

00:39:32.710 --> 00:39:36.840
takes our reactions and keeps us engaged,

00:39:36.840 --> 00:39:39.430
engaged, I'm beginning to hate that word,

00:39:39.430 --> 00:39:42.080
by playing to the worst of human nature,

00:39:42.080 --> 00:39:44.140
by playing to the devil in us.

00:39:44.140 --> 00:39:47.730
So part of Duterte's
power comes from the fact

00:39:47.730 --> 00:39:49.930
that not only does he
say those rich peoples,

00:39:49.930 --> 00:39:51.880
'cause one of the narratives they divided,

00:39:51.880 --> 00:39:54.640
the first division was
between the rich and the poor,

00:39:54.640 --> 00:39:56.340
which is really huge in the Philippines,

00:39:56.340 --> 00:40:01.333
but what he did is he
promised the poor revenge,

00:40:02.271 --> 00:40:03.810
a different thing.

00:40:03.810 --> 00:40:05.890
The thinking fast part is the part.

00:40:05.890 --> 00:40:07.530
And that's part of the reason I think

00:40:07.530 --> 00:40:11.830
that this is built by design.

00:40:11.830 --> 00:40:15.183
We are mildly addicted with
elevated levels of dopamine,

00:40:16.680 --> 00:40:19.340
you have elevated levels of oxytocin

00:40:19.340 --> 00:40:21.963
so that you stay and you share.

00:40:23.060 --> 00:40:26.520
So all of this must change,
but I think part of it

00:40:26.520 --> 00:40:30.393
is because the tech guys had full rein.

00:40:31.905 --> 00:40:36.905
For law if there is no rule of law,

00:40:37.200 --> 00:40:40.530
there is no democracy also
and that's part of the problem

00:40:40.530 --> 00:40:44.587
because in the virtual world,
there is no rule of law.

00:40:46.220 --> 00:40:51.040
And online violence leads
to real world violence.

00:40:51.040 --> 00:40:53.040
We know that also, those are clear.

00:40:53.040 --> 00:40:54.460
So that's part of the reason

00:40:54.460 --> 00:40:57.340
I think the world is upside
down does that makes sense?

00:40:57.340 --> 00:40:58.513
We gotta fix the tech.

00:41:00.140 --> 00:41:02.710
- Thank you, and now, Sheila Marcelo,

00:41:02.710 --> 00:41:07.290
COO of Care, Yale parent,
Harvard and (mumbles).

00:41:07.290 --> 00:41:08.840
- [Sheila] Maria, thank
you for doing this.

00:41:08.840 --> 00:41:11.770
You're such a good friend
and I am such a huge fan

00:41:11.770 --> 00:41:13.320
for your heroic efforts

00:41:13.320 --> 00:41:16.060
and what you're doing for
people and freedom of speech.

00:41:16.060 --> 00:41:18.610
And so when I think about, Ron and I,

00:41:18.610 --> 00:41:22.170
were just at the last World
Economic Forum at Davos

00:41:22.170 --> 00:41:24.850
and recently also just with some friends.

00:41:24.850 --> 00:41:27.330
And we were talking about the debate,

00:41:27.330 --> 00:41:30.520
the pressures on Mark and
Sheryl, as they think about it,

00:41:30.520 --> 00:41:33.720
and at Davos, we heard a debate
that New York Times hosted

00:41:33.720 --> 00:41:37.200
between regulation of big
data and self-regulation,

00:41:37.200 --> 00:41:39.350
like how should we
really think about that?

00:41:40.760 --> 00:41:43.050
So if you had Mark in front of you,

00:41:43.050 --> 00:41:44.580
which I know you've had opportunities

00:41:44.580 --> 00:41:46.540
to chat with him directly,

00:41:46.540 --> 00:41:48.370
from a self-regulation perspective,

00:41:48.370 --> 00:41:49.740
because I keep hearing from friends,

00:41:49.740 --> 00:41:53.380
the argument that it's a
lose-lose situation for him.

00:41:53.380 --> 00:41:54.980
Because that if he regulated

00:41:54.980 --> 00:41:58.330
and became the arbiter of
what was lies or truth,

00:41:58.330 --> 00:42:00.540
that's not really the
technology that you he built

00:42:00.540 --> 00:42:03.170
nor does he believe that that is his role.

00:42:03.170 --> 00:42:05.550
And so what for a business person,

00:42:05.550 --> 00:42:07.440
are the personal responsibility

00:42:07.440 --> 00:42:09.060
given that you've studied this so well.

00:42:09.060 --> 00:42:11.590
Like if you were talking to him today,

00:42:11.590 --> 00:42:13.570
what would be your advice to him

00:42:13.570 --> 00:42:16.130
around what he could be actively doing

00:42:16.130 --> 00:42:19.000
for self-regulation in the technology?

00:42:19.000 --> 00:42:21.380
Because, again, I've heard the argument,

00:42:21.380 --> 00:42:22.620
because of the conservatives,

00:42:22.620 --> 00:42:24.060
he'll say, well, they're gonna attack me

00:42:24.060 --> 00:42:27.660
if I shut down all the
lies, they're going up.

00:42:27.660 --> 00:42:29.860
Is it really about
Liberals and Conservatives?

00:42:29.860 --> 00:42:32.490
So what would you say to Mark?

00:42:32.490 --> 00:42:35.470
- So I think even without doing anything,

00:42:35.470 --> 00:42:38.690
the platforms are already making choices,

00:42:38.690 --> 00:42:40.350
but the way they're making choices

00:42:40.350 --> 00:42:43.300
is they are rewarding lies,

00:42:43.300 --> 00:42:45.190
they're rewarding the manipulation.

00:42:45.190 --> 00:42:47.990
And this is documented
in the Mueller Report.

00:42:47.990 --> 00:42:51.010
The networks of disinformation,
Russian disinformation.

00:42:51.010 --> 00:42:52.740
Guys, you're manipulated.

00:42:52.740 --> 00:42:55.000
I mean, that's already a fact

00:42:55.000 --> 00:42:56.730
and the fact that we
don't know it's a fact,

00:42:56.730 --> 00:42:59.860
it's really scary because
it will happen again

00:42:59.860 --> 00:43:01.700
and it is happening again.

00:43:01.700 --> 00:43:02.760
That's first.

00:43:02.760 --> 00:43:04.320
The second I think is.

00:43:04.320 --> 00:43:07.320
So the excuse that I don't
wanna regulate speech,

00:43:07.320 --> 00:43:10.700
you're already doing it
because you took the old rules,

00:43:10.700 --> 00:43:14.060
you trash them and you
replace them with rules

00:43:14.060 --> 00:43:16.133
that are not friendly to democracy.

00:43:17.300 --> 00:43:19.450
Journalists spend all our lives

00:43:19.450 --> 00:43:22.363
learning how to make
boring facts interesting.

00:43:23.580 --> 00:43:25.090
We learn how to tell stories,

00:43:25.090 --> 00:43:28.170
it's really hard to keep your attention.

00:43:28.170 --> 00:43:30.730
But now in the age of social media,

00:43:30.730 --> 00:43:34.960
someone will just lie and
lace it with anger and hate

00:43:34.960 --> 00:43:38.270
and we just don't stand
a chance in distribution.

00:43:38.270 --> 00:43:42.423
So that's the second part, what solution?

00:43:43.420 --> 00:43:46.420
I think there was this
brief period of time

00:43:46.420 --> 00:43:48.140
when they had free reign,

00:43:48.140 --> 00:43:51.870
and I think it was when Instant
Articles was brought in.

00:43:51.870 --> 00:43:53.970
All the news groups were
brought into Facebook.

00:43:53.970 --> 00:43:56.420
Facebook from that time period,

00:43:56.420 --> 00:44:00.750
by 2016, became the world's
largest distributor of news.

00:44:00.750 --> 00:44:05.460
Not coincidentally, 2016 is
when the dominoes began to fall.

00:44:05.460 --> 00:44:09.830
In May, the election of Duterte,
a month later was Brexit,

00:44:09.830 --> 00:44:12.050
Trump was November,
then you go all the way

00:44:12.050 --> 00:44:15.430
to the Catalonia elections that year.

00:44:15.430 --> 00:44:18.480
Because we found bots that were working

00:44:18.480 --> 00:44:20.790
in both the US and Catalonia elections

00:44:20.790 --> 00:44:23.600
tweeting exclusively
about the Philippines.

00:44:23.600 --> 00:44:25.583
So we know this is happening.

00:44:27.620 --> 00:44:29.900
I think it takes leadership,

00:44:29.900 --> 00:44:33.040
I think it's enlightened self interest.

00:44:33.040 --> 00:44:36.043
Because if they don't change course,

00:44:38.740 --> 00:44:40.440
an ambassador would know this, right?

00:44:40.440 --> 00:44:42.860
Whatever you do, you will be attacked,

00:44:42.860 --> 00:44:44.120
but you have to do the right thing.

00:44:44.120 --> 00:44:46.803
So the question back to him
is, what are your values?

00:44:47.870 --> 00:44:49.780
What are the values that govern this?

00:44:49.780 --> 00:44:51.920
It's almost like they created a city

00:44:51.920 --> 00:44:54.993
and gave everyone a machine
gun and then said, go at it.

00:44:56.017 --> 00:45:00.950
And Mad Max wins, that
isn't the way society works.

00:45:00.950 --> 00:45:02.650
And it filters to reality

00:45:02.650 --> 00:45:07.650
because online, virtual world is reality.

00:45:07.670 --> 00:45:09.570
The millennials here know that, right?

00:45:10.960 --> 00:45:12.710
That's the reason why I
think it will take time.

00:45:12.710 --> 00:45:14.720
A solution?
I've been thinking about this

00:45:14.720 --> 00:45:16.550
and we talked about this
at lunch a little bit

00:45:16.550 --> 00:45:20.420
is data portability, a potential
one, short-term solution.

00:45:20.420 --> 00:45:22.863
Because I don't wanna break up Facebook,

00:45:23.810 --> 00:45:27.070
even though a lot of the
legislations looking at antitrust.

00:45:27.070 --> 00:45:28.100
I don't wanna break 'em up

00:45:28.100 --> 00:45:29.750
because they did have the advantage,

00:45:29.750 --> 00:45:32.720
but how do you help younger groups

00:45:32.720 --> 00:45:36.680
like Jimmy Wales's new
social media platform?

00:45:36.680 --> 00:45:40.510
What if we give Facebook
incentive to protect the users?

00:45:40.510 --> 00:45:42.810
'Cause right now, every
incentive they have

00:45:42.810 --> 00:45:46.000
is actually to continue
going down this path

00:45:46.000 --> 00:45:47.733
to the destruction of democracy.

00:45:49.370 --> 00:45:51.400
They spent more money
protecting our privacy,

00:45:51.400 --> 00:45:54.593
they were pummeled by the
market, they lost a lot of money.

00:45:55.696 --> 00:45:59.570
So what if a user like me,
if I get attacked a lot,

00:45:59.570 --> 00:46:02.710
I can tell take all the data
I created on the platform

00:46:02.710 --> 00:46:06.280
and poured it over to Jimmy
Wales' new social media platform

00:46:06.280 --> 00:46:08.250
And I can take my family
and friends with me

00:46:08.250 --> 00:46:10.120
'cause I'll do that.

00:46:10.120 --> 00:46:14.140
They will protect us if they
will lose the network effect.

00:46:14.140 --> 00:46:16.610
So I think that's part of
what we have to think about.

00:46:16.610 --> 00:46:19.450
But the reason why I don't
think self-regulation works

00:46:19.450 --> 00:46:23.110
is 'cause they had the chance
and they didn't take it.

00:46:23.110 --> 00:46:26.700
So it goes back to what are the values?

00:46:26.700 --> 00:46:31.700
I think tech is not neutral, at any level.

00:46:36.070 --> 00:46:39.383
- Thank you, next question
please, young man at the back.

00:46:44.390 --> 00:46:45.573
- Thanks a lot, Maria.

00:46:46.600 --> 00:46:48.350
Did you--
- Stand up for me please.

00:46:48.350 --> 00:46:49.183
- [Kyle] Sure.

00:46:50.450 --> 00:46:52.820
Hi, how are you?
My name's Kyle.

00:46:52.820 --> 00:46:53.840
- You referred to Facebook

00:46:53.840 --> 00:46:55.580
as a distribution platform,

00:46:55.580 --> 00:46:59.030
and so I was wondering if these lies

00:46:59.030 --> 00:47:02.630
and the deceit and the
manipulation that you referred to.

00:47:02.630 --> 00:47:04.220
I was wondering if this has occurred

00:47:04.220 --> 00:47:06.930
on previous platforms in media history.

00:47:06.930 --> 00:47:08.497
Did this happen when the
television came to be?

00:47:08.497 --> 00:47:11.350
Did this happen through radio,

00:47:11.350 --> 00:47:13.100
in the printing press and so forth?

00:47:14.442 --> 00:47:18.630
And if so, how did we
regulate it in the past?

00:47:18.630 --> 00:47:21.130
- So I think we've never been at a stage

00:47:21.130 --> 00:47:24.830
where the rate of spread is exponential.

00:47:24.830 --> 00:47:26.990
This is truly game-changing, right?

00:47:26.990 --> 00:47:28.830
You're talking just Facebook alone,

00:47:28.830 --> 00:47:30.690
and I have to say, we're frenemies

00:47:30.690 --> 00:47:31.843
'cause we're partners of Facebook.

00:47:31.843 --> 00:47:33.880
I don't sound like it, do I?

00:47:33.880 --> 00:47:35.590
I know the good it can do

00:47:35.590 --> 00:47:37.600
which is part of the
reason I get disappointed

00:47:37.600 --> 00:47:40.180
when it doesn't do the good it can do.

00:47:40.180 --> 00:47:45.180
So, television, this printing press.

00:47:45.960 --> 00:47:48.490
Yes, there's something
different with each one,

00:47:48.490 --> 00:47:53.090
but it has never been at the edge

00:47:53.090 --> 00:47:57.070
of a human's ability to ingest.

00:47:57.070 --> 00:47:58.890
Part of it is, it's like the difference

00:47:58.890 --> 00:48:02.170
between an Excel sheet and big data.

00:48:02.170 --> 00:48:03.690
The difference in scale

00:48:03.690 --> 00:48:07.110
creates a completely
different world, right?

00:48:07.110 --> 00:48:08.560
So when you're doing big data,

00:48:08.560 --> 00:48:09.810
you can see all these patterns,

00:48:09.810 --> 00:48:12.313
you won't be able to see
in a small Excel Sheet.

00:48:13.700 --> 00:48:17.360
I think that the the tech platforms

00:48:17.360 --> 00:48:20.680
have enabled so many good things

00:48:20.680 --> 00:48:24.140
but at the same time,
they've also enabled the rise

00:48:24.140 --> 00:48:27.000
of these populist authoritarian leaders

00:48:27.000 --> 00:48:29.320
that are using these same platforms

00:48:30.330 --> 00:48:32.670
to use information operations
against their own people,

00:48:32.670 --> 00:48:35.470
to change democracy and this is a stat.

00:48:35.470 --> 00:48:38.780
In 2017, Freedom House came
out with the first study

00:48:38.780 --> 00:48:41.970
that said that in at least 28
countries around the world,

00:48:41.970 --> 00:48:45.440
cheap armies and social media
were rolling back democracy.

00:48:45.440 --> 00:48:47.480
Last year, Oxford University's

00:48:47.480 --> 00:48:49.660
Computational Propaganda Research Project

00:48:49.660 --> 00:48:54.380
said that number is now
at least 70 countries.

00:48:54.380 --> 00:48:56.210
So what do we do?

00:48:56.210 --> 00:48:59.697
Well, what has happened in
every industry, tech will say,

00:48:59.697 --> 00:49:03.860
"Look, this is a really
difficult industry to regulate."

00:49:03.860 --> 00:49:08.540
Is it any more difficult
than the airline industry

00:49:08.540 --> 00:49:12.560
or finance or pharmaceuticals?

00:49:12.560 --> 00:49:16.270
We have regulated very complex industries

00:49:16.270 --> 00:49:21.270
and you never allow a drug
makers to test the drug in public

00:49:22.590 --> 00:49:26.650
and say, let the user beware
or let me get them to say,

00:49:26.650 --> 00:49:29.440
okay, and then they can get poisoned.

00:49:29.440 --> 00:49:31.500
That's kind of what's happening, right?

00:49:31.500 --> 00:49:33.140
The onus is on the user

00:49:33.140 --> 00:49:36.620
that you're supposed to opt out or opt in.

00:49:36.620 --> 00:49:39.030
It's not about opting out or opting in,

00:49:39.030 --> 00:49:41.870
it's about knowing that
when I get on a plane,

00:49:41.870 --> 00:49:43.950
I don't have to know how the plane works

00:49:43.950 --> 00:49:46.270
to know the I'll be safe in the plane.

00:49:46.270 --> 00:49:48.750
That's what governments
are supposed to do, right?

00:49:48.750 --> 00:49:50.590
I didn't really answer your question

00:49:50.590 --> 00:49:52.400
because I think we're at that phase

00:49:52.400 --> 00:49:54.150
where we're looking for the answer.

00:49:54.150 --> 00:49:56.730
And I'll connect it to one thing

00:49:56.730 --> 00:49:59.863
which is similar to the
end of World War Two.

00:50:00.900 --> 00:50:03.993
At the end of World War Two,
the atom bomb scared everybody.

00:50:05.560 --> 00:50:09.320
Hitler scared everyone
and as a global community,

00:50:09.320 --> 00:50:14.320
we came together to create
Bretton Woods, NATO,

00:50:14.840 --> 00:50:16.830
the UN Declaration of Human Rights,

00:50:16.830 --> 00:50:19.380
that we decided that we
need to protect humanity

00:50:19.380 --> 00:50:21.603
from the worst of what human beings do.

00:50:22.590 --> 00:50:25.250
We're at the same juncture today.

00:50:25.250 --> 00:50:28.900
And information is the problem now, right?

00:50:28.900 --> 00:50:32.660
But we don't agree that
an atom bomb went off.

00:50:32.660 --> 00:50:36.580
So the entire system has
already gone up and smokes,

00:50:36.580 --> 00:50:38.720
but we don't even realize it.

00:50:38.720 --> 00:50:39.913
That's scary.

00:50:41.940 --> 00:50:43.700
Should there be Christopher Wiley?

00:50:43.700 --> 00:50:46.720
The Cambridge analytical
whistleblower said that,

00:50:46.720 --> 00:50:47.880
in his book he recommended,

00:50:47.880 --> 00:50:50.873
there should be building
codes for tech platforms.

00:50:51.740 --> 00:50:54.340
There should be ethical codes.

00:50:54.340 --> 00:50:56.910
Tristan Harris, Head of Humane Tech,

00:50:56.910 --> 00:50:59.223
he was Google's ethicist at some point.

00:51:00.300 --> 00:51:02.000
The other thing Wiley said is,

00:51:02.000 --> 00:51:06.150
every individual coder
should be legally liable

00:51:06.150 --> 00:51:08.643
for the code that they create.

00:51:10.720 --> 00:51:13.320
I'm just saying this is a brave new world.

00:51:13.320 --> 00:51:14.560
No altruism, right?

00:51:14.560 --> 00:51:18.060
It's a brave new world and
we have to like grapple,

00:51:18.060 --> 00:51:23.060
call a spade a spade
and demand protection.

00:51:23.640 --> 00:51:27.310
As a journalist, the Philippines,
in the United States,

00:51:27.310 --> 00:51:30.560
our Constitution is patterned
after the United States.

00:51:30.560 --> 00:51:32.750
I'm protected under the Constitution

00:51:32.750 --> 00:51:35.000
of both of our countries and yet,

00:51:35.000 --> 00:51:37.140
all of that protection was stripped

00:51:37.140 --> 00:51:39.343
the minute I entered the virtual world.

00:51:41.016 --> 00:51:43.010
- Thank you, next question please.

00:51:43.010 --> 00:51:43.843
Yes, sir.

00:51:51.170 --> 00:51:55.020
- [Bob] My name is Bob
and my question is this.

00:51:55.020 --> 00:51:57.430
If we're going to change course,

00:51:57.430 --> 00:51:59.560
we're going to need new narratives.

00:51:59.560 --> 00:52:03.590
My question is, where do these
new narratives come from?

00:52:03.590 --> 00:52:05.983
And maybe what might be in the them?

00:52:09.440 --> 00:52:11.516
- I wish Harry (mumbles)
would answer that question.

00:52:11.516 --> 00:52:13.110
(Maria laughing)

00:52:13.110 --> 00:52:15.110
The new directions I think,

00:52:15.110 --> 00:52:18.810
the one word that I don't
hear a lot in tech is values

00:52:20.950 --> 00:52:24.750
which underpins America.

00:52:24.750 --> 00:52:29.750
It underpins every society
that pushes for progress.

00:52:31.779 --> 00:52:33.123
So I think the first is,

00:52:34.360 --> 00:52:37.560
you can see I'm very
influenced by social media

00:52:37.560 --> 00:52:40.880
because I grew Rappler from zero

00:52:40.880 --> 00:52:43.690
to being the third-top
online news website,

00:52:43.690 --> 00:52:45.723
we're the top digital only website.

00:52:47.010 --> 00:52:49.660
And I can see that the
incentives are all skewed

00:52:49.660 --> 00:52:51.710
because it happened so fast,

00:52:51.710 --> 00:52:53.480
the rush to grow these platforms.

00:52:53.480 --> 00:52:57.440
So tech for me is something
we cannot discount.

00:52:57.440 --> 00:53:00.210
I also don't think we should kill tech.

00:53:00.210 --> 00:53:04.000
Sheila built a business
on using tech, right?

00:53:04.000 --> 00:53:08.740
Tech does enable, but when
we see something wrong

00:53:08.740 --> 00:53:11.113
for business men in particular,

00:53:13.020 --> 00:53:16.930
there has to be some
stop, some accountability.

00:53:16.930 --> 00:53:18.400
So what to do?

00:53:18.400 --> 00:53:21.400
Obviously, our political systems
have already been affected.

00:53:23.120 --> 00:53:25.070
I came with something very simple,

00:53:25.070 --> 00:53:28.020
which is in your area of influence,

00:53:28.020 --> 00:53:32.840
please demand what you believe,
what values you stand for,

00:53:32.840 --> 00:53:34.920
what you believe should happen.

00:53:34.920 --> 00:53:39.920
Make facts important because
everyone jumps to thinking fast

00:53:40.220 --> 00:53:42.600
instead of thinking slow.

00:53:42.600 --> 00:53:47.110
Shares, our generation is
the one that shares lies

00:53:47.110 --> 00:53:49.573
seven times more than the millennials,

00:53:50.430 --> 00:53:53.710
because we grew in a
world of trust, right?

00:53:53.710 --> 00:53:56.540
In a way, these social
media platforms benefited

00:53:56.540 --> 00:53:58.650
from the trust that's embedded

00:53:58.650 --> 00:54:02.520
in the way we build our
businesses and communities.

00:54:02.520 --> 00:54:05.720
And now we're going to have to
rebuild that and rethink it.

00:54:05.720 --> 00:54:07.493
I'm sorry, it's a fudgy answer.

00:54:09.214 --> 00:54:13.740
Maybe our esteemed Ambassador,
do you have an idea?

00:54:13.740 --> 00:54:14.810
Find a new leader.

00:54:14.810 --> 00:54:15.752
No, sorry.

00:54:15.752 --> 00:54:16.830
(everybody laughing)

00:54:16.830 --> 00:54:18.300
Oops.

00:54:18.300 --> 00:54:19.940
- I wouldn't go there, I'm retired now,

00:54:19.940 --> 00:54:23.160
I can say whatever I would
like but I would say the truth,

00:54:23.160 --> 00:54:26.427
justice values still reign
and they are so important

00:54:26.427 --> 00:54:31.427
and we cannot abdicate that
under any circumstances

00:54:32.880 --> 00:54:35.118
and we cannot become transactional.

00:54:35.118 --> 00:54:36.568
(mumbles) you had a question.

00:54:39.420 --> 00:54:40.270
Just call me sir.

00:54:48.980 --> 00:54:51.160
- [Steven] My name is Steven,
thank you for being here.

00:54:51.160 --> 00:54:55.130
I saw the documentary on
cable, very impressive for you.

00:54:57.270 --> 00:55:01.630
My problem is this, I'll make this short,

00:55:01.630 --> 00:55:04.320
I've been leading nonprofit
for the last close to 10 years

00:55:04.320 --> 00:55:06.680
and I worked with
Egyptian students in Cairo

00:55:06.680 --> 00:55:09.680
when our spring occurred and
I work with Ukrainian students

00:55:09.680 --> 00:55:11.800
and I work with Syrian students.

00:55:11.800 --> 00:55:16.093
And the problem is, they end
up all preaching to the choir.

00:55:17.100 --> 00:55:18.120
They don't reach out,

00:55:18.120 --> 00:55:21.640
Ukraine has done that
more than the other two.

00:55:21.640 --> 00:55:23.660
But the Arab Spring failed in Egypt

00:55:23.660 --> 00:55:26.130
because they were just
talking to themselves.

00:55:26.130 --> 00:55:29.110
And my question to you is,

00:55:29.110 --> 00:55:33.060
are you reaching out to people
who are not in your camp?

00:55:33.060 --> 00:55:35.370
Because I happen to know

00:55:35.370 --> 00:55:39.230
more than a few Filipinos in New York,

00:55:39.230 --> 00:55:42.500
and they tell me, they know about Duterte

00:55:42.500 --> 00:55:44.370
and they know it is craziness or whatever,

00:55:44.370 --> 00:55:45.750
but they say, You know what?

00:55:45.750 --> 00:55:48.441
He's done a lot for us.

00:55:48.441 --> 00:55:50.190
Yes, he's killed a lot of people,

00:55:50.190 --> 00:55:53.453
but a lot of those people are
drug war lords or whatever.

00:55:55.090 --> 00:55:57.120
Do I care about Maria Ressa?

00:55:57.120 --> 00:56:01.690
Yes, but the bottom line is,
Duterte's done so much for us

00:56:01.690 --> 00:56:03.960
that I'll just ignore it.

00:56:03.960 --> 00:56:05.560
It doesn't really matter to me.

00:56:05.560 --> 00:56:07.380
I just want Duterte to continue,

00:56:07.380 --> 00:56:11.090
we have a much more stable society.

00:56:11.090 --> 00:56:14.610
That's like it is here
with Trump and his appeal.

00:56:14.610 --> 00:56:16.540
So my question to you is,

00:56:16.540 --> 00:56:20.410
are you really appealing to
people who are not of your ilk?

00:56:20.410 --> 00:56:23.810
And if so, let us know.

00:56:23.810 --> 00:56:24.920
Thank you.

00:56:24.920 --> 00:56:25.870
- Thanks for your question.

00:56:25.870 --> 00:56:30.870
So number one, is that I
never was of an ilk, right?

00:56:31.460 --> 00:56:34.110
I mean, I'm a journalist

00:56:34.110 --> 00:56:37.270
and I actually really did live with this.

00:56:37.270 --> 00:56:42.270
The problem now is, when
my rights were violated,

00:56:42.510 --> 00:56:44.510
I had first-hand knowledge

00:56:44.510 --> 00:56:47.630
of how the government abused its powers

00:56:47.630 --> 00:56:50.320
and I decided then to take a step forward

00:56:50.320 --> 00:56:52.780
into something that's
pretty uncharted for me.

00:56:52.780 --> 00:56:55.370
I talked about the injustice that I saw.

00:56:55.370 --> 00:56:59.400
I was unshackled and I
hold government to account

00:56:59.400 --> 00:57:02.780
because my Government
abused its power, right?

00:57:02.780 --> 00:57:04.860
I'm not taking a side, I never have.

00:57:04.860 --> 00:57:07.273
I'm not a political
animal, I'm a journalist.

00:57:09.650 --> 00:57:11.720
The Liberal Party in the Philippines

00:57:11.720 --> 00:57:13.800
will say part of the
reason Duterte got elected

00:57:13.800 --> 00:57:16.180
is because Rappler covered him

00:57:16.180 --> 00:57:19.480
and gave a presidential debate.

00:57:19.480 --> 00:57:23.680
We covered all five camps
equally and continue to do that.

00:57:23.680 --> 00:57:28.680
Unfortunately, we refuse to
stop doing investigative reports

00:57:29.570 --> 00:57:32.180
and you can say, oh,
it feels really great.

00:57:32.180 --> 00:57:33.960
It's okay to kill a few people,

00:57:33.960 --> 00:57:37.750
which by the way, AstroTurf
messages on social media,

00:57:37.750 --> 00:57:42.200
which is meant to change
the way people think.

00:57:42.200 --> 00:57:45.480
So that happens but at the same time,

00:57:45.480 --> 00:57:50.070
look this year, foreign
direct investment is down 31%.

00:57:50.070 --> 00:57:53.140
If you look at the facts, the
facts say a different thing,

00:57:53.140 --> 00:57:55.660
but what happens when facts don't matter?

00:57:55.660 --> 00:57:57.210
Take a look at the Philippines.

00:57:58.430 --> 00:58:01.670
Facts have to match because in the end,

00:58:01.670 --> 00:58:04.940
we negotiate everything
in a public sphere.

00:58:04.940 --> 00:58:06.730
And I need to listen.

00:58:06.730 --> 00:58:10.220
By no means do I think
I have any solution.

00:58:10.220 --> 00:58:12.730
I guess that's the best
part of being a reporter.

00:58:12.730 --> 00:58:17.180
You want to get the best
knowledge from everyone else

00:58:17.180 --> 00:58:19.120
so you can put the best thing together.

00:58:19.120 --> 00:58:20.870
Running a company is similar to that.

00:58:20.870 --> 00:58:23.510
That's the kind of role I play in Rappler,

00:58:23.510 --> 00:58:26.540
I try to get the best ideas
and we pull them together.

00:58:26.540 --> 00:58:28.550
That's what democracy should be.

00:58:28.550 --> 00:58:31.799
It isn't about one side yelling at another

00:58:31.799 --> 00:58:34.350
or saying this is better than yours.

00:58:34.350 --> 00:58:35.240
Which by the way,

00:58:35.240 --> 00:58:39.700
came from inciting a mob
in the public sphere.

00:58:39.700 --> 00:58:41.880
This is information warfare.

00:58:41.880 --> 00:58:44.330
So I go back to that, yes, I do.

00:58:44.330 --> 00:58:47.690
Number two, we reach out all the time.

00:58:47.690 --> 00:58:51.523
Many of the propagandists in
that network that I showed you

00:58:53.930 --> 00:58:56.790
were part of our civic engagement team.

00:58:56.790 --> 00:58:57.990
And it is so painful

00:58:57.990 --> 00:59:01.053
to see some of these
tactics then being used.

00:59:02.740 --> 00:59:05.380
Before this time as a
reporter, as a journalist,

00:59:05.380 --> 00:59:09.540
I've never used good versus
evil in the way I do now.

00:59:09.540 --> 00:59:13.130
I think the advice I gave last
night to some Yale students

00:59:13.130 --> 00:59:17.020
is that the values are so important now.

00:59:17.020 --> 00:59:20.820
I think while you're still
in school, define that line.

00:59:20.820 --> 00:59:22.500
Where on this side, you're good

00:59:22.500 --> 00:59:24.270
and on this side, you're evil.

00:59:24.270 --> 00:59:26.540
Because the world is complex

00:59:26.540 --> 00:59:30.130
and it literally manipulates you.

00:59:30.130 --> 00:59:33.850
So be clear who you are, what
are the values you stand for?

00:59:33.850 --> 00:59:35.200
And I think that's the best thing.

00:59:35.200 --> 00:59:40.030
So my quick response is I
continue trying to reach out,

00:59:40.030 --> 00:59:41.830
but when I'm vilified

00:59:41.830 --> 00:59:45.410
and portrayed exponentially
as a tax evader,

00:59:45.410 --> 00:59:46.560
even though my company

00:59:46.560 --> 00:59:49.660
was given the top corporate tax awards

00:59:49.660 --> 00:59:51.020
six months before they filed

00:59:51.020 --> 00:59:53.743
five tax evasion cases against us.

00:59:55.830 --> 00:59:58.233
I get angry so I learned anger management.

01:00:00.160 --> 01:00:02.280
- Thank you, next question please.

01:00:02.280 --> 01:00:03.743
Yes, right behind.

01:00:06.940 --> 01:00:08.060
- [Sarah] Hi, my name is Sarah.

01:00:08.060 --> 01:00:10.010
I'm a second year student
at the law school here.

01:00:10.010 --> 01:00:12.530
So it appears that your
seemed to be more in favor

01:00:12.530 --> 01:00:14.510
of stricter regulation within big tech

01:00:14.510 --> 01:00:16.270
and social media platforms.

01:00:16.270 --> 01:00:18.750
So what is your opinion
on stricter media laws?

01:00:18.750 --> 01:00:20.090
So for example,

01:00:20.090 --> 01:00:23.130
for demonstrably false
information i.e fake news,

01:00:23.130 --> 01:00:25.180
do you think that should be criminalized?

01:00:26.400 --> 01:00:27.930
- One of the things we're fighting against

01:00:27.930 --> 01:00:30.760
in the Philippines is that
libel is a criminal offense.

01:00:30.760 --> 01:00:35.010
The cyber libel means that
the penalty is double,

01:00:35.010 --> 01:00:38.910
normal libel and I face criminal charges.

01:00:38.910 --> 01:00:42.123
So we've long been against
criminalizing libel.

01:00:44.360 --> 01:00:47.690
So here's another complexity
to add to the layer.

01:00:47.690 --> 01:00:49.670
Our old world was complex enough,

01:00:49.670 --> 01:00:51.990
when you add the layer of tech.

01:00:51.990 --> 01:00:53.640
Look at a country like Singapore.

01:00:54.690 --> 01:00:57.500
Singapore is about 5 million people,

01:00:57.500 --> 01:00:59.080
Harry can talk more about this,

01:00:59.080 --> 01:01:02.160
but Singapore has 10-year plans

01:01:02.160 --> 01:01:05.130
and they're actually very efficient.

01:01:05.130 --> 01:01:08.340
It's a democracy not in the way we are.

01:01:08.340 --> 01:01:11.730
They pass the first fake news law.

01:01:11.730 --> 01:01:15.450
Maybe second, Malaysia was
first in Southeast Asia.

01:01:15.450 --> 01:01:19.090
And now it's being used against folks

01:01:19.090 --> 01:01:22.403
who are perceived to be
critical of government.

01:01:24.280 --> 01:01:27.360
There isn't a one size fits all answer,

01:01:27.360 --> 01:01:30.030
and there certainly isn't a quid pro quo.

01:01:30.030 --> 01:01:32.480
If we regulate tech,
should we regulate media?

01:01:32.480 --> 01:01:35.000
If we regulate media,
should we not regulate?

01:01:35.000 --> 01:01:36.400
There isn't anything like that,

01:01:36.400 --> 01:01:37.830
these are false equivalences.

01:01:37.830 --> 01:01:41.560
I think for me, all I'm
trying to do is to go back

01:01:41.560 --> 01:01:45.430
to how do we protect the public sphere?

01:01:45.430 --> 01:01:47.740
How do we take the new technology,

01:01:47.740 --> 01:01:50.160
machine-learning, artificial intelligence.

01:01:50.160 --> 01:01:53.500
These platforms know you more intimately

01:01:53.500 --> 01:01:55.830
than your most intimate relationships,

01:01:55.830 --> 01:01:58.850
because you put that data in there.

01:01:58.850 --> 01:02:01.943
It's not content, it's behavior, right?

01:02:03.120 --> 01:02:06.220
So I'm trying to understand the world

01:02:07.090 --> 01:02:09.190
and trying to stay out of jail (laughing).

01:02:10.700 --> 01:02:12.010
- Thank you, next question.

01:02:12.010 --> 01:02:12.933
Yes, young man.

01:02:13.930 --> 01:02:14.763
Patient.

01:02:15.840 --> 01:02:16.917
Walking (speaking off mic).

01:02:21.080 --> 01:02:23.730
- [Greg] Hi I'm Greg, I'm a
student here from Indonesia.

01:02:23.730 --> 01:02:25.710
And I know that you were in Jakarta

01:02:25.710 --> 01:02:27.210
during the democratic transition

01:02:27.210 --> 01:02:29.160
between Suharto and the reform period,

01:02:29.160 --> 01:02:30.760
and in Manila be fore that.

01:02:30.760 --> 01:02:34.590
So having seen and reported
on a lot of authoritarians,

01:02:34.590 --> 01:02:36.940
how is it different now
or is it just the same?

01:02:40.140 --> 01:02:42.962
- I started my career in 1986,

01:02:42.962 --> 01:02:45.310
34 years ago with People Power,

01:02:45.310 --> 01:02:48.700
and then I was very privileged with CNN,

01:02:48.700 --> 01:02:52.790
they gave me a front-row
seat right when I felt like,

01:02:52.790 --> 01:02:57.690
oh my gosh, these changes from
authoritarian one man rulers,

01:02:57.690 --> 01:03:00.800
Singapore, Lee Kuan Yew to Goh Chok Tong,

01:03:00.800 --> 01:03:02.690
Mahathir stepping down

01:03:02.690 --> 01:03:05.133
and now of course he was
back and now he stepped down.

01:03:06.090 --> 01:03:07.310
And then Indonesia,

01:03:07.310 --> 01:03:11.173
the end of almost 32 years
of Suharto was incredible.

01:03:12.865 --> 01:03:13.850
I felt like the pendulum,

01:03:13.850 --> 01:03:17.210
like a pendulum was released
and it was swinging back.

01:03:17.210 --> 01:03:21.390
And I think the painful
part for me three years ago

01:03:21.390 --> 01:03:23.960
was to see that pendulum go back.

01:03:23.960 --> 01:03:28.820
And went back because of the
in formation ecosystem, right?

01:03:28.820 --> 01:03:32.260
So Indonesia has similar problems.

01:03:32.260 --> 01:03:34.960
The top Twitter city in
the world is Jakarta.

01:03:34.960 --> 01:03:38.470
And let's look back to the days of Suharto

01:03:38.470 --> 01:03:42.223
when the violence and unbond in 1999.

01:03:43.739 --> 01:03:45.400
10, 000 people were killed

01:03:45.400 --> 01:03:50.400
because one newspaper printed
something that was incendiary.

01:03:50.950 --> 01:03:55.170
Grenade was thrown in a
mosque and the killers,

01:03:55.170 --> 01:03:56.003
do you remember?

01:03:56.003 --> 01:03:58.786
This was before you were
born, how old are you?

01:03:58.786 --> 01:04:00.127
(Maria laughing)

01:04:00.127 --> 01:04:01.813
- Born '99.
- Yay.

01:04:03.910 --> 01:04:07.240
But so I remember then
how difficult that was

01:04:07.240 --> 01:04:09.030
and that was only a newspaper.

01:04:09.030 --> 01:04:13.810
And then we saw the riots, the
Jakarta riots again recently

01:04:13.810 --> 01:04:18.750
and Indonesia actually had
to a hawk, those are sorry,

01:04:18.750 --> 01:04:22.693
you know this better
than I do that ethnic,

01:04:24.140 --> 01:04:27.653
communal violence, the
time I was in Jakarta,

01:04:28.730 --> 01:04:31.920
I went through all 27
at that point provinces,

01:04:31.920 --> 01:04:34.310
and I think I covered every single

01:04:34.310 --> 01:04:36.700
form of conflict in a town.

01:04:36.700 --> 01:04:39.430
From ethnic violence from, the (mumbles),

01:04:39.430 --> 01:04:41.350
the beheadings that happen there.

01:04:41.350 --> 01:04:46.350
This was in 1997 from
Archer separatism, violence.

01:04:47.680 --> 01:04:51.763
It's a tinderbox and I
worry about social media.

01:04:53.250 --> 01:04:56.363
Sorry, I ran off into
where I wanted to go.

01:04:58.160 --> 01:05:01.270
I worry about the information ecosystem.

01:05:01.270 --> 01:05:06.270
I think that when you
manipulate us emotionally,

01:05:06.550 --> 01:05:08.810
we have no defenses against it.

01:05:08.810 --> 01:05:11.740
And when it is done for power,

01:05:11.740 --> 01:05:14.023
we cannot hold our governments to account.

01:05:17.900 --> 01:05:19.640
- [Ron] So just a quick follow up.

01:05:19.640 --> 01:05:22.910
Your walked down, thank
you again, always an honor.

01:05:22.910 --> 01:05:25.083
- You're such a familiar, thank you.

01:05:27.140 --> 01:05:29.653
- [Ron] I'm Ron, I'm Sheila's husband.

01:05:30.820 --> 01:05:32.870
And your walk down memory lane

01:05:32.870 --> 01:05:35.330
made me think of a term,
"puppet government",

01:05:35.330 --> 01:05:38.700
which I thought the general
public of most of those nations

01:05:38.700 --> 01:05:40.320
that were under puppet governments,

01:05:40.320 --> 01:05:45.320
recognized the influence
of money behind the leaders

01:05:46.530 --> 01:05:49.380
and again, the general public
so the majority of the people.

01:05:49.380 --> 01:05:54.380
And now in this age, my
question really is about,

01:05:54.600 --> 01:05:58.820
that term in my opinion,
doesn't seem to be used anymore

01:05:58.820 --> 01:06:03.530
because seems this big money
has somehow in new systems,

01:06:03.530 --> 01:06:08.410
gotten somehow to a tipping
point our country as an example,

01:06:08.410 --> 01:06:11.680
where it's sort of mass
the influence of big money

01:06:11.680 --> 01:06:15.540
to command sort of what
we're seeing in policy

01:06:15.540 --> 01:06:16.980
and the outcomes.

01:06:16.980 --> 01:06:18.270
Could you comment a little

01:06:18.270 --> 01:06:22.590
about how to make that big
money more transparent today?

01:06:22.590 --> 01:06:25.850
That again, I would say, the
majority of people would say,

01:06:25.850 --> 01:06:29.210
that's not a good thing to
have big money influencing us

01:06:29.210 --> 01:06:32.213
in this way through
these funded campaigns.

01:06:33.690 --> 01:06:36.330
So for example, the Marcoses
are still billionaires.

01:06:36.330 --> 01:06:38.040
Some people in this room
might not know that,

01:06:38.040 --> 01:06:40.023
but that is still a fact.

01:06:42.580 --> 01:06:45.420
- ICIJ, are you guys familiar with ICIJ?

01:06:45.420 --> 01:06:48.240
International Center for
Investigative Journalism?

01:06:48.240 --> 01:06:51.820
There's huge data that
has been released globally

01:06:51.820 --> 01:06:54.550
and journalists in many
different parts of the world

01:06:54.550 --> 01:06:56.960
come together to work on this data.

01:06:56.960 --> 01:07:00.770
Some of that data exposes
the Marcos wealth.

01:07:00.770 --> 01:07:03.290
The Panama Papers if
you're familiar with that,

01:07:03.290 --> 01:07:06.620
that came from ICIJ's collaborative work.

01:07:06.620 --> 01:07:08.090
What you really made me think of

01:07:08.090 --> 01:07:10.970
and I guess I went off on
an aside from your question

01:07:10.970 --> 01:07:13.500
was the geopolitical power,

01:07:13.500 --> 01:07:16.720
'cause beyond the corporations
is actually the money

01:07:16.720 --> 01:07:19.090
that, I see this in the Philippines,

01:07:19.090 --> 01:07:21.360
the money that's flowing
in the Philippines

01:07:21.360 --> 01:07:23.723
that is helping change geopolitical power.

01:07:25.540 --> 01:07:28.880
China, Russia, United States,

01:07:28.880 --> 01:07:31.970
the Philippines is pivotal,
always has been, right?

01:07:31.970 --> 01:07:34.880
And in October 2016 when President Duterte

01:07:34.880 --> 01:07:36.680
went to Beijing on a state visit

01:07:36.680 --> 01:07:40.250
and he didn't tell his
Foreign Affairs Secretary,

01:07:40.250 --> 01:07:43.380
that he announced that the
Philippines would move away

01:07:43.380 --> 01:07:47.330
from the United States,
pivot to China and Russia.

01:07:47.330 --> 01:07:50.210
That was the first time I
heard Russia in Beijing.

01:07:50.210 --> 01:07:52.163
So that money has come in.

01:07:53.210 --> 01:07:57.140
Badly, we know this, and
we're trying to track that.

01:07:57.140 --> 01:08:00.290
Morality the rebuilding of
morality in the Philippines

01:08:00.290 --> 01:08:02.600
is Chinese right supposedly,

01:08:02.600 --> 01:08:04.350
but the rebuilding hasn't happened.

01:08:05.960 --> 01:08:07.310
So I'm just saying that the money

01:08:07.310 --> 01:08:11.970
is a money flow of governments
for geopolitical power,

01:08:11.970 --> 01:08:15.250
and then you're talking
about the kind of money

01:08:15.250 --> 01:08:17.003
that families would have.

01:08:18.110 --> 01:08:19.730
What we're seeing right
now in the Philippines

01:08:19.730 --> 01:08:21.863
is the building of a new kleptocracy.

01:08:23.470 --> 01:08:25.320
And you're seeing it in plain sight,

01:08:25.320 --> 01:08:26.610
because it's two years

01:08:26.610 --> 01:08:29.420
before the end of
President Duterte's term.

01:08:29.420 --> 01:08:30.990
He has one six-year term,

01:08:30.990 --> 01:08:35.140
and they're now getting
ready for elections next,

01:08:35.140 --> 01:08:37.260
campaigns will begin next year.

01:08:37.260 --> 01:08:39.700
So what's happening is
the blue chip companies,

01:08:39.700 --> 01:08:43.100
the largest conglomerate in the
Philippines, the Ayala Group,

01:08:43.100 --> 01:08:45.890
has been forced to
essentially sell it's shares

01:08:45.890 --> 01:08:49.680
to businessmen chosen who are
friendly to President Duterte.

01:08:49.680 --> 01:08:51.780
The kleptocracy is
happening in plain sight.

01:08:51.780 --> 01:08:54.143
Harry you should talk more this.

01:08:55.100 --> 01:08:57.470
So yes, the flow of money is important.

01:08:57.470 --> 01:08:58.980
Can it be more transparent?
Yes.

01:08:58.980 --> 01:09:01.020
Has it ever been transparent
in the Philippines?

01:09:01.020 --> 01:09:01.853
Never.

01:09:03.980 --> 01:09:07.340
Which is why investigative
journalism is also important.

01:09:07.340 --> 01:09:10.780
We're trying to do more
it, it's scary for me

01:09:10.780 --> 01:09:15.120
that you have the lobbyist
group, a conglomerate,

01:09:15.120 --> 01:09:18.590
the Ayala Group, the First
Pacific Group, Manny Pangilinan.

01:09:18.590 --> 01:09:21.000
these are three big conglomerates
who are being forced,

01:09:21.000 --> 01:09:25.483
like the Philippine Daily
Inquirer, to sell their shares.

01:09:26.740 --> 01:09:28.040
For money to change hands,

01:09:28.040 --> 01:09:31.000
because again, a
kleptocracy is being built.

01:09:31.000 --> 01:09:33.020
We're not the first country
where there's a kleptocracy,

01:09:33.020 --> 01:09:35.253
so all these words oligarchs,

01:09:36.280 --> 01:09:40.763
the names change but the
system remains the same.

01:09:42.120 --> 01:09:43.850
- Thank you, young lady.

01:09:43.850 --> 01:09:44.713
Right here, yes.

01:09:47.900 --> 01:09:50.520
- [Marianne] Hello, Marianne,

01:09:50.520 --> 01:09:52.087
I'm a senior from the Philippines--

01:09:52.087 --> 01:09:55.483
- And her father is one of
the investors of Rappler.

01:09:56.365 --> 01:09:59.448
(everybody laughing)

01:10:00.900 --> 01:10:02.513
Sorry, I didn't mean.

01:10:03.670 --> 01:10:05.690
- [Marianne] I was wondering,

01:10:05.690 --> 01:10:08.090
so it seems like the world
that we're living in,

01:10:09.160 --> 01:10:12.690
well, the Philippines Marcosian

01:10:12.690 --> 01:10:14.490
kind of like media censorship

01:10:15.360 --> 01:10:17.610
seems to be rearing its
ugly head once again,

01:10:17.610 --> 01:10:19.900
like, you've had all of these lawsuits

01:10:19.900 --> 01:10:22.130
and arrest warrants against you.

01:10:22.130 --> 01:10:25.280
I think just last week, the
National Police brought up

01:10:25.280 --> 01:10:28.030
that Presidential Decree
number 90 under Marcos,

01:10:28.030 --> 01:10:29.540
which is about like rumor mongering,

01:10:29.540 --> 01:10:34.220
which is defunct but they're
kind of returning to that

01:10:34.220 --> 01:10:35.950
and this whole thing with ABS CBN,

01:10:35.950 --> 01:10:38.110
one of the major national networks

01:10:38.110 --> 01:10:42.130
on the verge of being closed down.

01:10:42.130 --> 01:10:44.910
So I was just wondering
how you are navigating

01:10:44.910 --> 01:10:47.113
that kind of tricky space.

01:10:49.730 --> 01:10:51.230
- It's like a game of chicken.

01:10:52.210 --> 01:10:55.503
You just keep going down the right path.

01:10:57.210 --> 01:10:59.330
So my lawyers, I asked my lawyers this.

01:10:59.330 --> 01:11:01.380
Right before we came in here,
I was on with my lawyers.

01:11:01.380 --> 01:11:03.660
So I asked my lawyers
this, what can they do?

01:11:03.660 --> 01:11:06.923
They can do anything,
that's really the reality.

01:11:07.790 --> 01:11:09.943
I could be charged with child abuse.

01:11:11.900 --> 01:11:14.390
And I guess my best defense,

01:11:14.390 --> 01:11:17.290
my only defense is to shine the light.

01:11:17.290 --> 01:11:20.400
To tell you the story, to
tell you what's happening.

01:11:20.400 --> 01:11:22.800
I think the reason why
Rappler is still alive

01:11:22.800 --> 01:11:26.620
is because one, we're owned
largely by journalists.

01:11:26.620 --> 01:11:29.060
We decided that we would hold the line

01:11:29.060 --> 01:11:33.520
and then two, we asked
international news groups

01:11:33.520 --> 01:11:35.130
to tell our story.

01:11:35.130 --> 01:11:37.040
And it reverberates

01:11:37.040 --> 01:11:40.730
because it's happening in many
countries around the world.

01:11:40.730 --> 01:11:42.590
So I guess part of it is,

01:11:42.590 --> 01:11:44.350
they always say don't
sweat the small stuff.

01:11:44.350 --> 01:11:46.530
It's now the small stuff is so big.

01:11:46.530 --> 01:11:49.380
It's like, oh, I could go to
jail, I won't think about it.

01:11:49.380 --> 01:11:51.740
I'll just focus on where I need to go.

01:11:51.740 --> 01:11:54.170
And that works for now.

01:11:54.170 --> 01:11:57.850
That's why I'm also hoping
our allies, the tech allies,

01:11:57.850 --> 01:11:59.400
now frenemies, remember?

01:11:59.400 --> 01:12:00.500
I keep appealing.

01:12:00.500 --> 01:12:04.610
So I work very closely
with the tech companies,

01:12:04.610 --> 01:12:07.983
because they're really
critical for the first step.

01:12:09.310 --> 01:12:10.970
What does civic engagement look like

01:12:10.970 --> 01:12:13.573
in the age of social media for your group?

01:12:16.070 --> 01:12:17.990
That's a long-winded answer.

01:12:17.990 --> 01:12:22.440
I think the short answer is,
we look at people like you.

01:12:22.440 --> 01:12:26.580
We are developing the next
generation of journalists.

01:12:26.580 --> 01:12:30.463
This time requires far more
courage than at any other time.

01:12:31.940 --> 01:12:36.940
And you at Yale are so
privileged and powerful.

01:12:37.570 --> 01:12:42.570
And please like the thinking-slow
world, I so long for that,

01:12:43.990 --> 01:12:46.723
because we can't move
forward without that.

01:12:52.740 --> 01:12:54.400
- [Joe] My name is Joe, Joe (mumbles),

01:12:54.400 --> 01:12:56.820
I'm a Filipino-American.

01:12:56.820 --> 01:12:59.390
I'm an officer of the Association here

01:12:59.390 --> 01:13:01.900
so I'm part of the community.

01:13:01.900 --> 01:13:05.450
And I'm also part of the battleground

01:13:05.450 --> 01:13:08.110
because as part of the community,

01:13:08.110 --> 01:13:12.380
I know what the gentleman
here said a while ago.

01:13:12.380 --> 01:13:17.380
There are so many pro-Duterte
Filipino-Americans as well.

01:13:19.450 --> 01:13:23.483
And it feels to me like
it was even harder now.

01:13:24.370 --> 01:13:27.130
I was also a anti-Marcos activist

01:13:27.130 --> 01:13:29.350
in my years in the University
of the Philippines.

01:13:29.350 --> 01:13:32.640
It was even easier then than it is now.

01:13:32.640 --> 01:13:34.160
I can easily reason

01:13:34.160 --> 01:13:39.160
with pro-Marcos supporters
before in the 80s

01:13:39.270 --> 01:13:43.960
than to do it with
pro-Duterte supporters now,

01:13:43.960 --> 01:13:48.300
and many of them are my
former colleagues in the left.

01:13:48.300 --> 01:13:49.483
So we were split.

01:13:51.020 --> 01:13:54.890
We are coming to 2022, that's
the presidential elections.

01:13:54.890 --> 01:13:58.090
We were clobbered in social media.

01:13:58.090 --> 01:14:03.090
So it was very hard to do the
debates or to win in debates.

01:14:04.590 --> 01:14:09.590
What is your outlook of 2022
in terms of the battlefield,

01:14:12.030 --> 01:14:14.950
that means the information battlefield?

01:14:14.950 --> 01:14:17.240
Because we will be in there again,

01:14:17.240 --> 01:14:19.860
we will be debating with our friends

01:14:19.860 --> 01:14:22.220
who are with the other camp,

01:14:22.220 --> 01:14:25.350
and we were clobbered because
they were so organized

01:14:25.350 --> 01:14:29.250
and we were coming from different places.

01:14:29.250 --> 01:14:33.020
How are we gonna win this,
if you have any ideas,

01:14:33.020 --> 01:14:35.713
for people like us who
are in the community?

01:14:36.600 --> 01:14:40.670
- I think it's impossible
to win the social media war

01:14:41.600 --> 01:14:44.513
unless you have no values.

01:14:45.390 --> 01:14:49.303
The only way to win this is
it's a scorched-earth policy.

01:14:51.470 --> 01:14:54.460
It's the use of anger and hate to spread

01:14:54.460 --> 01:14:56.803
and you demonize someone else.

01:14:58.320 --> 01:15:00.910
It's a world that I don't believe in

01:15:00.910 --> 01:15:03.070
which is why when Mark Zuckerberg says,

01:15:03.070 --> 01:15:07.040
we don't want to be the arbiters
of truth, you already are.

01:15:07.040 --> 01:15:10.840
They're prioritizing speech
that incites the hate

01:15:10.840 --> 01:15:15.550
and violence more than they
prioritize facts, right?

01:15:15.550 --> 01:15:17.180
This is built into the system.

01:15:17.180 --> 01:15:19.150
So I think the first thing is,

01:15:19.150 --> 01:15:21.030
I called it a behavioral
modification system,

01:15:21.030 --> 01:15:23.170
but think about it like this.

01:15:23.170 --> 01:15:26.080
In this system that they've created,

01:15:26.080 --> 01:15:30.150
they decided that they wanted
to grow it fast, so fast,

01:15:30.150 --> 01:15:31.920
there's a cost to growing it fast.

01:15:31.920 --> 01:15:33.970
So a tech person made the decision

01:15:33.970 --> 01:15:37.870
that they're going to
use friends of friends,

01:15:37.870 --> 01:15:41.120
because if it's friends of
friends, just that one decision,

01:15:41.120 --> 01:15:44.353
you'll be able to close triangles,

01:15:45.260 --> 01:15:47.960
there's a paper by Mark Granovetter

01:15:47.960 --> 01:15:50.530
called The Strength of Weak Ties.

01:15:50.530 --> 01:15:52.090
So they close these triangles,

01:15:52.090 --> 01:15:57.090
but in doing that, they build
polarization into that system.

01:15:57.980 --> 01:16:01.060
Because friends of friends,
so think about like this,

01:16:01.060 --> 01:16:03.320
if you have rightist tendencies,

01:16:03.320 --> 01:16:05.000
like in our world maybe it was like this,

01:16:05.000 --> 01:16:06.470
in the Philippines it was like this,

01:16:06.470 --> 01:16:08.290
in the US you had Fox and CNN

01:16:08.290 --> 01:16:11.790
so you had your own media things.

01:16:11.790 --> 01:16:14.090
But in the Philippines we were like this.

01:16:14.090 --> 01:16:16.470
So if I had rightist tendencies

01:16:16.470 --> 01:16:18.050
and I only talked to my friends

01:16:18.050 --> 01:16:21.120
and grew my network of friends,
then I moved further here.

01:16:21.120 --> 01:16:22.890
And then if I had leftist tendencies

01:16:22.890 --> 01:16:24.670
and I only talked to all my friends

01:16:24.670 --> 01:16:26.150
and grew my own network of friends,

01:16:26.150 --> 01:16:28.910
I move further here, you
never see the other side.

01:16:28.910 --> 01:16:32.443
Eli Pariser wrote a book
about The Filter Bubbles,

01:16:33.400 --> 01:16:37.150
this is in the design,
so how do we do that?

01:16:37.150 --> 01:16:41.380
It's not about conversation or
even listening to each other

01:16:41.380 --> 01:16:42.980
or convincing each other.

01:16:42.980 --> 01:16:45.090
In order to convince someone,

01:16:45.090 --> 01:16:46.570
you have to listen to each other.

01:16:46.570 --> 01:16:48.800
That's not built into the design.

01:16:48.800 --> 01:16:52.520
So I've seen good people

01:16:52.520 --> 01:16:57.520
use the exact same black
strategy as the Duterte camp.

01:16:58.030 --> 01:17:03.030
And you know what, it leaves
a trail and we can see that.

01:17:03.120 --> 01:17:06.040
I don't think that's the
right way to go either.

01:17:06.040 --> 01:17:08.840
That's part of the reason
I think we need to demand

01:17:08.840 --> 01:17:13.840
that the platforms change
the way they create a system,

01:17:14.690 --> 01:17:17.870
because they talk about a market of ideas,

01:17:17.870 --> 01:17:19.570
a market has rules.

01:17:19.570 --> 01:17:23.040
You check out here., you
don't bring a gun in, right?

01:17:23.040 --> 01:17:26.320
You have to have rules for
a marketplace of ideas.

01:17:26.320 --> 01:17:28.410
Because right now,

01:17:28.410 --> 01:17:33.410
you can't insure that election
integrity even exists.

01:17:33.490 --> 01:17:37.723
We can't, because the information
ecosystem is so flawed.

01:17:38.800 --> 01:17:40.396
- Thank you, so very much.

01:17:40.396 --> 01:17:42.676
Salama (background noise
drowns the speaker)

01:17:42.676 --> 01:17:45.676
(audience clapping)

01:17:50.242 --> 01:17:53.409
(lively upbeat music)

